"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkdocusaurus_blog_rudevico=globalThis.webpackChunkdocusaurus_blog_rudevico||[]).push([[114],{4776:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"a-day-of-kakao-problems","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/a-day-of-kakao-problems","editUrl":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam/blog/2026-11-06-a-day-of-kakao-problems/index.md","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-06-a-day-of-kakao-problems/index.md","title":"The Day Kakao Gave Me a Beating","description":"Getting stuck counts as growth, right?","date":"2026-11-06T07:05:22.829Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Technology","permalink":"/en/tags/tech"}],"readingTime":1.79,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"HyoYoonNam","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam","page":null,"description":"Java, Spring\uc744 \uba54\uc778\uc73c\ub85c \ud558\ub294 \ucde8\uc900\uc0dd\uc785\ub2c8\ub2e4.","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam/","github":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam","email":"mailto:dev.rudevico@gmail.com"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam.png","key":"hyoyoonnam"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"a-day-of-kakao-problems","title":"The Day Kakao Gave Me a Beating","date":"2026-11-06T07:05:22.829Z","authors":"hyoyoonnam","tags":["\uae30\uc220"],"image":"../../../../blog/2026-11-06-a-day-of-kakao-problems/hide-the-cactus.png"},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Hiding Debug Output When You Submit a Solution","permalink":"/en/hide-debug-output-on-submission"}},"content":"## Getting stuck counts as growth, right?\\n\\n![image.png](../../../../blog/2026-11-06-a-day-of-kakao-problems/hide-the-cactus.png)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The source of my heartburn\\n\\n![17bcf00719bce84c1058d82030c7fe08307e20d2.heic](../../../../blog/2026-11-06-a-day-of-kakao-problems/photo-1.jpg)\\n\\n## Please don\u2019t cross that river\\n\\nI looked up some Level 1 problems I\u2019d solved more than a year ago. Back then their solve rates had been low. They\u2019d gone up quite a bit since.\\n\\n![9f16ab6e49b557894e94c39a26da45c22d0aff1e.heic](../../../../blog/2026-11-06-a-day-of-kakao-problems/photo-2.jpg)\\n\\nI figured the same thing had happened with Hide the Cactus.\\\\\\nI really should\u2019ve left that one alone\u2026\\n\\n## Time complexity\\n\\nI\u2019ll reconstruct the problem roughly from memory. Absolutely nothing to do with being afraid to look at it again.\\n\\n* 1 &lt;= m, n &lt;= 500_000\\n* 1 &lt;= m x n &lt;= 500_000\\n\\nGiven those constraints, even a loop over m with a loop over n inside it would stay within a reasonable bound. \u201cWell, I can just brute-force this,\u201d I thought.\\n\\nThen my very humble solution (does it count as a solution if I never solved it?) grew to four nested loops, and suddenly I was looking at `250_000_000_000`.\\n\\n\u201cI\u2019ll come up with something so ingenious even Ludens will fall over backwards.\u201d I turned it over every which way. What actually fell into a ditch was two hours of my time.\\n\\n![2fd49a82ca3d4bc0b6da2b998aeaa165ed8b23e2.jpeg](../../../../blog/2026-11-06-a-day-of-kakao-problems/reaction.jpg)\\n\\nI tried an easier Kakao problem after that. I don\u2019t know. The problem itself seemed fine. My head and whatever was going on inside it were another story.\\\\\\nOne more failed attempt, and then the eleven o\u2019clock ride home from campus.\\n\\nWhere did my time go?\\n\\n## Next time, cactus\\n\\nLudens looked through a few blogs and told me this one called for a monotonic queue. I\u2019ve done a few monotonic stack problems before. I wonder how much carries over? A few more stack problems, and next week I\u2019ll come back and give that cactus what for.\\n\\n![image.png](../../../../blog/2026-11-06-a-day-of-kakao-problems/next-problem.png)\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Original post on Rilog (Korean)](https://www.rilog.kr/@homo_scribens/posts/512)"},{"id":"hide-debug-output-on-submission","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/hide-debug-output-on-submission","editUrl":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam/blog/2026-10-30-hide-debug-output-on-submission/index.md","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-30-hide-debug-output-on-submission/index.md","title":"Hiding Debug Output When You Submit a Solution","description":"cover","date":"2026-10-29T23:56:14.559Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Technology","permalink":"/en/tags/tech"}],"readingTime":2.24,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"HyoYoonNam","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam","page":null,"description":"Java, Spring\uc744 \uba54\uc778\uc73c\ub85c \ud558\ub294 \ucde8\uc900\uc0dd\uc785\ub2c8\ub2e4.","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam/","github":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam","email":"mailto:dev.rudevico@gmail.com"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam.png","key":"hyoyoonnam"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"hide-debug-output-on-submission","title":"Hiding Debug Output When You Submit a Solution","date":"2026-10-29T23:56:14.559Z","authors":"hyoyoonnam","tags":["\uae30\uc220"],"image":"../../../../blog/2026-10-30-hide-debug-output-on-submission/cover.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"The Day Kakao Gave Me a Beating","permalink":"/en/a-day-of-kakao-problems"},"nextItem":{"title":"I Solved a Programmers Problem. It\u2019s Been a Year and a Half.","permalink":"/en/back-to-programmers"}},"content":"![cover](../../../../blog/2026-10-30-hide-debug-output-on-submission/cover.png)\\n\\nThe examples are in **Java**, taken from my solution to [Park Walk on Programmers](https://school.programmers.co.kr/learn/courses/30/lessons/172928).\\n\\n**What you\u2019ll take away**\\n\\n```text\\nWhen a problem gets confusing, you can print as much intermediate state as you need\\nand still keep those lines out of the output the judge sees.\\n```\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The short version\\n\\nDefine the `print(String format, Object... args)` helper below and use it wherever you want to inspect a value.\\n\\n```java\\n// Example 1\\nprint(\\"Can move? now=%s(%d, %d)%n\\", mat[x][tempY], x, tempY);\\nprint(\\"Move allowed! now=%s(%d, %d)%n\\", mat[x][tempY], x, tempY);\\nprint(\\"Move (Y): %d -> %d%n\\", y, tempY);\\n// Example 2\\nprint(\\"=== step %d (start_%s) | oper_%s ===%n\\", count++, start, oper);\\n\\n\\npublic static final boolean DEBUG_MODE = false;\\n\\nprivate static void print(String format, Object... args) {\\n    if (DEBUG_MODE) System.out.printf(format, args);\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## What I was struggling with\\n\\nI kept getting my bearings wrong at first: how far did I need to search, and from which starting point? I wanted to see the intermediate results, so I started with plain `System.out.println()` calls.\\n\\nI soon got tired of <u>typing the same output method over and over</u> and <u>joining strings with</u> `+` <u>just to inspect a value</u>. I wrote `print()` to take care of both.\\n\\n```java\\nprivate static void print(String format, Object... args) {\\n    System.out.printf(format, args);\\n}\\n```\\n\\n* A short helper takes care of <u>typing the same method repeatedly</u>.\\n* `printf()` and the varargs parameter `Object... args` take care of <u>assembling debug messages with string concatenation</u>.\\n\\n## An excerpt from my solution\\n\\n```java\\n    public static char[][] mat = new char[55][55];\\n    public static int ROW_SIZE;\\n    public static int COL_SIZE;\\n\\n    public int[] solution(\\n        String[] park, // Each cell contains S, O, or X.\\n        String[] routes // Commands for the dog, in the form \\"op n\\": direction and number of squares\\n    ) {\\n        ROW_SIZE = park.length - 1;\\n        COL_SIZE = park[0].length() - 1;\\n\\n        Pos start = new Pos();\\n        print(\\"<ROW_SIZE=%d, COL_SIZE=%d>%n\\", ROW_SIZE, COL_SIZE);\\n        for (int row = 0; row < ROW_SIZE + 1; row++) {\\n            for (int col = 0; col < COL_SIZE + 1; col++) {\\n                char target = park[row].charAt(col);\\n                mat[row][col] = target;\\n                if (target == \'S\') start = new Pos(row, col);\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        for (int row = 0; row < ROW_SIZE + 1; row++) {\\n            for (int col = 0; col < COL_SIZE + 1; col++) {\\n                print(\\"%s \\", mat[row][col]);\\n            }\\n            print(\\"%n\\");\\n        }\\n\\n        int count = 1;\\n        for (String oper : routes) {\\n            print(\\"=== step %d (start_%s) | oper_%s ===%n\\", count++, start, oper);\\n            if (start.canMoveTo(oper)) {\\n                start.move(oper);\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        int[] answer = {start.x, start.y}; // Dog\u2019s final position after all commands: [row, column]\\n        return answer;\\n    }\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Original post on Rilog (Korean)](https://www.rilog.kr/@homo_scribens/posts/328)"},{"id":"back-to-programmers","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/back-to-programmers","editUrl":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam/blog/2026-10-28-back-to-programmers/index.md","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-28-back-to-programmers/index.md","title":"I Solved a Programmers Problem. It\u2019s Been a Year and a Half.","description":"cover","date":"2026-10-27T15:32:08.750Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Technology","permalink":"/en/tags/tech"}],"readingTime":3.27,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"HyoYoonNam","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam","page":null,"description":"Java, Spring\uc744 \uba54\uc778\uc73c\ub85c \ud558\ub294 \ucde8\uc900\uc0dd\uc785\ub2c8\ub2e4.","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam/","github":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam","email":"mailto:dev.rudevico@gmail.com"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam.png","key":"hyoyoonnam"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"back-to-programmers","title":"I Solved a Programmers Problem. It\u2019s Been a Year and a Half.","date":"2026-10-27T15:32:08.750Z","authors":"hyoyoonnam","tags":["\uae30\uc220"],"image":"../../../../blog/2026-10-28-back-to-programmers/cover.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Hiding Debug Output When You Submit a Solution","permalink":"/en/hide-debug-output-on-submission"},"nextItem":{"title":"A Moment of Compassion","permalink":"/en/a-moment-of-compassion"}},"content":"![cover](../../../../blog/2026-10-28-back-to-programmers/cover.jpg)\\n\\n## A little digression\\n\\n### Consistency is a weapon\\n\\n<figure>\\n\\n![ordinary twig](../../../../blog/2026-10-28-back-to-programmers/ordinary-twig.png)\\n\\n<figcaption>Ordinary Twig. A perfectly ordinary-looking twig made through alchemy. It doesn\u2019t seem to have any special powers. (<a href=\\"https://zyquest.tistory.com/270\\">Image source</a>)</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n\u21b3 And there\u2019s my weapon.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n![image.png](../../../../blog/2026-10-28-back-to-programmers/solving-history.png)\\n\\nMy history shows I was solving problems until about the *summer of 2025*. After that, nothing. Back to square zero, then?\\n\\n### Could Rudevico have been a frontend developer?\\n\\n![image.png](../../../../blog/2026-10-28-back-to-programmers/javascript-history.png)\\n\\nApparently I solved some in JS, too. Was that when I was preparing for Naver Boostcamp? I remember how exciting it felt just to be using a new language. At the time, the missions that landed every day were so\u2026 incredibly\u2026 hard. I was forever staying up all night and crashing at four in the afternoon. I was a wreck. Funny how many good memories I have of it now.\\n\\n## The first problem on my return\\n\\nI went with `Running Race | Practice | Level 1`.\\n\\n![image.png](../../../../blog/2026-10-28-back-to-programmers/running-race.png)\\n\\n### My solution\\n\\n```java\\nimport java.util.*;\\n\\nclass Solution {\\n    // A runner\u2019s name is called when they overtake someone.\\n    public String[] solution(\\n        String[] players, // Runners in their current order, starting with first place\\n        String[] callings // Names in the order they are called\\n    ) {\\n        Map<String, Integer> playerOrderMap = new HashMap<>();\\n        for (int i = 0; i < players.length; i++) {\\n            playerOrderMap.put(players[i], i);\\n        }\\n\\n        // 1. Call a name.\\n        for (String calling : callings) {\\n            int order = playerOrderMap.get(calling); // Runner in second, for example\\n            String temp = players[order-1]; // Runner in first\\n\\n            players[order-1] = players[order]; // Move the second runner into first.\\n            players[order] = temp; // Move the former leader into second.\\n\\n            playerOrderMap.put(calling, order - 1); // Overtook\\n            playerOrderMap.put(temp, order); // Was overtaken\\n        }\\n\\n\\n        // Names in finishing order, from first through nth\\n        return players;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Looking back\\n\\nAt first I skipped the `Map` and tried `for (String calling : callings) { brute-force : players }`. It timed out. So far, fine. I suspected I\u2019d need a map anyway. A quick solution I expected to fail would tell me whether I was approaching the problem the right way.\\n\\n#### Where I went wrong\\n\\nThen I kept getting the swap wrong. All I wanted to do was use `temp` to exchange the runners in positions n and n\u22121.\\n\\n```java\\nplayerOrderMap.put(calling, order - 1); // Overtook\\n// Fix: temp still holds the runner who occupied [order-1].\\nplayerOrderMap.put(temp, order); // Was overtaken\\n// Bug: [order-1] has already changed, but I treated it as if it still held the previous runner.\\n// playerOrderMap.put(players[order-1], order);\\n```\\n\\n* I\u2019d written `playerOrderMap.put(players[order-1], order)` where I needed `playerOrderMap.put(temp, order)`. The original runner in position n\u22121 dropped out of the ordering, and the runner from position n appeared twice.\\n\\nMaybe I was rusty. I had to print the whole thing out before I could see it.\\n\\n```shellscript\\nkai overtakes poe\\n# bug: poe disappears and kai appears twice\\nCurrent order: mumu,soe,kai,kai,mine,\\nkai overtakes kai\\n# bug: kai disappears and mine appears twice\\nCurrent order: mumu,soe,kai,kai,mine,\\nmine overtakes kai\\nCurrent order: mumu,soe,kai,mine,mine,\\nmine overtakes mine\\nCurrent order: mumu,soe,kai,mine,mine,\\n```\\n\\n#### Something for next time\\n\\nThere\u2019s a Duck in our group who\u2019s suspiciously good at these problems. I had a peek at their solution. While I was losing track of what `temp`, `players[order]`, and `players[order-1]` stood for, Duck had a variable called `frontName`. I liked that word, *front*. It told me who was standing where.\\n\\nDuck did say nice things about my solution. Whether that was praise or a way to fish a drowning newbie out by the collar and keep them from escaping the group\u2026 I\u2019m still not sure.\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Original post on Rilog (Korean)](https://www.rilog.kr/@homo_scribens/posts/313)"},{"id":"a-moment-of-compassion","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/a-moment-of-compassion","editUrl":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam/blog/2026-10-27-a-moment-of-compassion/index.md","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-27-a-moment-of-compassion/index.md","title":"A Moment of Compassion","description":"cover","date":"2026-10-26T20:57:30.920Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Technology","permalink":"/en/tags/tech"}],"readingTime":1.74,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"HyoYoonNam","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam","page":null,"description":"Java, Spring\uc744 \uba54\uc778\uc73c\ub85c \ud558\ub294 \ucde8\uc900\uc0dd\uc785\ub2c8\ub2e4.","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam/","github":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam","email":"mailto:dev.rudevico@gmail.com"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam.png","key":"hyoyoonnam"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"a-moment-of-compassion","title":"A Moment of Compassion","date":"2026-10-26T20:57:30.920Z","authors":"hyoyoonnam","tags":["\uae30\uc220"],"image":"../../../../blog/2026-10-27-a-moment-of-compassion/cover.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"I Solved a Programmers Problem. It\u2019s Been a Year and a Half.","permalink":"/en/back-to-programmers"},"nextItem":{"title":"I Bought Two Books and Felt Less Excited Than Usual","permalink":"/en/buying-two-books"}},"content":"![cover](../../../../blog/2026-10-27-a-moment-of-compassion/cover.png)\\n\\nWas it two or three weeks ago? For some reason I woke early and went for a run along the Tancheon. Dawn had passed, but the sunlight still looked blue. I\u2019d run an easy two kilometers or so and was heading back up.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThe paved ramp was broad enough for maintenance vehicles. Most of the time a metal barrier keeps them out, leaving just enough of a gap for one person to pass.\\n\\n[Photo of the ramp goes here]\\n\\nI\u2019d just finished my run and was puffing my way uphill. Behind me came a middle-aged man who looked as though he\u2019d been out for a leisurely walk.\\n\\nAs I reached the top, another middle-aged man on a bicycle went past me in the opposite direction. He looked a little younger than the man coming up.\\n\\nThey were both heading for the same gap. Only one could get through at a time. The man coming uphill was looking at his phone, and it seemed to fall to the cyclist to do enough dodging for the pair of them, in rather a hurry.\\n\\nHe must have steered well, because they didn\u2019t collide. He did mutter a startled \u201cAh, damn it\u201d on his way down.\\n\\nFor all his attention to the phone, the other man heard that part perfectly well. \u201cHey! What did you just say?\u201d Their voices rose.\\n\\n...\\n\\nWatching them, I felt sorry for them both. I\u2019m grateful whenever I get to go outside and enjoy the morning air. A surprising number of things have to come together for me to do it, from going to bed early the night before to getting past the urge to spend five more minutes under the covers. All that, spoiled for want of a moment\u2019s patience. I wondered whether they\u2019d spend the day complaining about each other to their families, or replaying it alone and getting upset all over again.\\n\\n![cover](../../../../blog/2026-10-27-a-moment-of-compassion/cover.png)\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Original post on Rilog (Korean)](https://www.rilog.kr/@homo_scribens/posts/288)"},{"id":"buying-two-books","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/buying-two-books","editUrl":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam/blog/2026-10-24-buying-two-books/index.md","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-24-buying-two-books/index.md","title":"I Bought Two Books and Felt Less Excited Than Usual","description":"postcard","date":"2026-10-23T15:27:35.344Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Reflections","permalink":"/en/tags/retrospective"}],"readingTime":2.5,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"HyoYoonNam","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam","page":null,"description":"Java, Spring\uc744 \uba54\uc778\uc73c\ub85c \ud558\ub294 \ucde8\uc900\uc0dd\uc785\ub2c8\ub2e4.","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam/","github":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam","email":"mailto:dev.rudevico@gmail.com"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam.png","key":"hyoyoonnam"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"buying-two-books","title":"I Bought Two Books and Felt Less Excited Than Usual","date":"2026-10-23T15:27:35.344Z","authors":"hyoyoonnam","tags":["\ud68c\uace0"],"image":"../../../../blog/2026-10-24-buying-two-books/postcard.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"A Moment of Compassion","permalink":"/en/a-moment-of-compassion"},"nextItem":{"title":"A Wife and Children Are a Great Hindrance to Great Undertakings","permalink":"/en/on-marriage-and-single-life"}},"content":"![postcard](../../../../blog/2026-10-24-buying-two-books/postcard.jpg)\\n\\n## I bought a book.\\n\\nVolume one of Haruki Murakami\u2019s *The Wind-Up Bird Chronicle*.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<figure>\\n\\n![IMG_2332.jpeg](../../../../blog/2026-10-24-buying-two-books/wind-up-bird.jpg)\\n\\n<figcaption>Volume one of The Wind-Up Bird Chronicle, from an Aladin secondhand bookshop.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\nI got it secondhand for 4,900 won. Isn\u2019t that ridiculously cheap? I was happy. What made me happier still was that it was a **well-aged book**.\\n\\n<figure>\\n\\n![IMG_2333.jpeg](../../../../blog/2026-10-24-buying-two-books/postcard.jpg)\\n\\n<figcaption>Tucked inside was a postcard that expired in 2008.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\nOh, and I bought a fabric book cover. Was it 10,800 won?\\n\\n## Except\u2026\\n\\nHere\u2019s where things went awry. Two beeps from the scanner, and the cashier said:\\n\\n> \u201cIf you spend 20,000 won, you get 3,000 won off. Shall I ring these up as they are?\u201d\\n\\nThis happened last time, too. Buying more to get the discount would cost me more than skipping it, I thought.\\n\\n> \u201cJust these, please.\u201d\\n\\nAnd then I went home.\\n\\n## So why\u2026?\\n\\nWhy was it that today I heard myself say,\\n\\n> \u201cOh, then I\u2019ll have another look around.\u201d\\n\\nWhere did that come from?\\n\\nOff I went to find a book that would bring me up to the threshold. If I was buying one, I wanted one I actually cared about. English and American fiction, Japanese fiction, popular science, the Minumsa World Literature shelves\u2026 I drifted from section to section.\\n\\nNothing. Nothing I felt drawn to.\\n\\nIt\u2019s hot in Aladin. I was wearing long sleeves, a lightweight puffer, and a trench coat. The trench coat had long since ended up over my arm. I thought my arm might give out if I added the puffer, so I wandered around with it half off, flapping behind me.\\n\\nHalf off, half overheated. My arm was heavy, and the rest of me was starting to feel it. Still no book. I\u2019d already sunk ten minutes into this. I finally took the puffer all the way off and browsed in slightly more comfort, but my body had already got the message.\\n\\nAnd so, suddenly worn out, I picked up Julian Barnes\u2019s *Departure(s)* and trudged home.\\n\\n<figure>\\n\\n![IMG_2334.jpeg](../../../../blog/2026-10-24-buying-two-books/departures.jpg)\\n\\n<figcaption>The book I finally settled on, Departure(s) by Julian Barnes.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\nWhen I pick a book, something about the title and the author\u2019s name has to catch me on sight. It has to look cool to me. This title is so much my sort of thing that I\u2019d had my eye on it for ages. How did it take me that long to spot it?\\n\\nWhen I started writing this I was still thinking, **what a waste of a trip to the bookshop**. Now I\u2019m looking at my two books and I feel rather pleased. Maybe it does come down to where you choose to end the story.\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Original post on Rilog (Korean)](https://www.rilog.kr/@homo_scribens/posts/264)"},{"id":"on-marriage-and-single-life","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/on-marriage-and-single-life","editUrl":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam/blog/2026-10-23-on-marriage-and-single-life/index.md","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-23-on-marriage-and-single-life/index.md","title":"A Wife and Children Are a Great Hindrance to Great Undertakings","description":"cover","date":"2026-10-22T16:43:06.876Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life","description":"Stories from rudevico\u2019s everyday life"}],"readingTime":1.61,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"HyoYoonNam","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam","page":null,"description":"Java, Spring\uc744 \uba54\uc778\uc73c\ub85c \ud558\ub294 \ucde8\uc900\uc0dd\uc785\ub2c8\ub2e4.","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam/","github":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam","email":"mailto:dev.rudevico@gmail.com"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam.png","key":"hyoyoonnam"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"on-marriage-and-single-life","title":"A Wife and Children Are a Great Hindrance to Great Undertakings","date":"2026-10-22T16:43:06.876Z","authors":"hyoyoonnam","tags":["life"],"image":"../../../../blog/2026-10-23-on-marriage-and-single-life/cover.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"I Bought Two Books and Felt Less Excited Than Usual","permalink":"/en/buying-two-books"},"nextItem":{"title":"When Indifference Fades | Fitzgerald\u2019s Winter Dreams","permalink":"/en/fading-indifference-in-winter-dreams"}},"content":"![cover](../../../../blog/2026-10-23-on-marriage-and-single-life/cover.png)\\n\\n> *A man with a wife and children has given hostages to fortune. In good undertakings and bad alike, they are a great hindrance to anyone who would do great things.* \u2014 [Francis Bacon, \u201cOf Marriage and Single Life,\u201d translated here from the Moonye edition](https://www.yes24.com/product/goods/147564050)\\n\\nQuite a provocative thing to say. Would I really concentrate harder on my work if I had no children? Couldn\u2019t the responsibility of having a child give me a reason to concentrate harder?\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nIt\u2019s exam season at university. I walk into the library determined to pull an all-nighter. It\u2019s ten in the evening, and my computer simulation class starts at two the next afternoon. Sixteen whole hours. Finishing just one chapter every three hours will do nicely.\\n\\n\u3030\ufe0f\u3030\ufe0f\\n\\nDo those sixteen hours I\u2019ve conjured up by staying awake all belong to me?\\n\\n\u3030\ufe0f\u3030\ufe0f\\n\\nAnd would every hour freed up by having no children be an hour I could spend concentrating? I doubt it. As I was saying, having a child might help me settle into something more deeply and do it well. Though if we\u2019re talking about a newborn who wakes, sleeps, cries, and fusses at all hours and completely wrecks my sleep\u2026 well, that would be hard.\\n\\nI like children, but I don\u2019t consciously go around thinking, \u201cI need a wife and kids.\u201d Maybe a child is someone who comes along in the course of living, one more person to share the journey with.\\n\\nIf I did share my life with a child, I\u2019d want to meet them as an equal. Another whole person living a life like mine and utterly different from it. That feels a little different from being what people call \u201ca parent who\u2019s like a friend.\u201d\\n\\n\u3030\ufe0f\u3030\ufe0f\\n\\nParent | Child | Lover | Friend | Teacher\\n\\nDifferent, and much the same.\\n\\n\u3030\ufe0f\u3030\ufe0f\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Original post on Rilog (Korean)](https://www.rilog.kr/@homo_scribens/posts/249)"},{"id":"fading-indifference-in-winter-dreams","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/fading-indifference-in-winter-dreams","editUrl":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam/blog/2026-10-21-fading-indifference-in-winter-dreams/index.md","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-21-fading-indifference-in-winter-dreams/index.md","title":"When Indifference Fades | Fitzgerald\u2019s Winter Dreams","description":"cover","date":"2026-10-21T12:57:32.019Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life","description":"Stories from rudevico\u2019s everyday life"}],"readingTime":1.28,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"HyoYoonNam","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam","page":null,"description":"Java, Spring\uc744 \uba54\uc778\uc73c\ub85c \ud558\ub294 \ucde8\uc900\uc0dd\uc785\ub2c8\ub2e4.","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam/","github":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam","email":"mailto:dev.rudevico@gmail.com"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam.png","key":"hyoyoonnam"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"fading-indifference-in-winter-dreams","title":"When Indifference Fades | Fitzgerald\u2019s Winter Dreams","date":"2026-10-21T12:57:32.019Z","authors":"hyoyoonnam","tags":["life"],"image":"../../../../blog/2026-10-21-fading-indifference-in-winter-dreams/cover.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"A Wife and Children Are a Great Hindrance to Great Undertakings","permalink":"/en/on-marriage-and-single-life"},"nextItem":{"title":"Does Elephant Actually Like Fish Cake? Reading Bacon on Parents and Children","permalink":"/en/on-parents-and-children"}},"content":"![cover](../../../../blog/2026-10-21-fading-indifference-in-winter-dreams/cover.png)\\n\\n> *As he saw it, she hadn\u2019t reproached him because she didn\u2019t want anything to dilute the complete indifference she\u2019d shown him, an indifference that had seemed quite genuine.* \u2014 F. Scott Fitzgerald, \u201cWinter Dreams,\u201d translated here from the Korean edition\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nI don\u2019t linger over books. I tend to skim along by feel, with no particular ambition to understand a book in its entirety. Every so often, though, I catch myself slowing down.\\n\\n\u3030\ufe0f\u3030\ufe0f\\n\\nIt happened when I came across the idea that **indifference could fade**. It stayed with me enough that [I even wrote down a thought about it](https://chaekchaek.com/download).\\n\\n<figure>\\n\\n![chaekchaek note](../../../../blog/2026-10-21-fading-indifference-in-winter-dreams/chaekchaek-note.png)\\n\\n<figcaption>The note I left on Chaekchaek about fading indifference.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\nThink of something you\u2019re indifferent to. Perhaps you\u2019ve never even noticed your indifference. A tissue fluttering in the air conditioning at a restaurant, say, or a gray ball of dust on the floor beside your desk.\\n\\nThen there\u2019s a moment when that indifference wears thin. The fluttering tissue suddenly starts to bother you. Someone walks past and the little ball of dust goes skittering off, and your eyes follow it\u2026\\n\\n\u3030\ufe0f\u3030\ufe0f\\n\\nI wonder if it\u2019s the same with people. Maybe what I feel first is my indifference fading.\\n\\n \u3030\ufe0f\u3030\ufe0f\\n\\n<figure>\\n\\n![IMG_2315.jpeg](../../../../blog/2026-10-21-fading-indifference-in-winter-dreams/winter-dreams.jpg)\\n\\n<figcaption>Fading indifference. The end.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Original post on Rilog (Korean)](https://www.rilog.kr/@homo_scribens/posts/231)"},{"id":"on-parents-and-children","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/on-parents-and-children","editUrl":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam/blog/2026-10-19-on-parents-and-children/index.md","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-19-on-parents-and-children/index.md","title":"Does Elephant Actually Like Fish Cake? Reading Bacon on Parents and Children","description":"cover","date":"2026-10-19T14:59:21.960Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life","description":"Stories from rudevico\u2019s everyday life"}],"readingTime":1.85,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"HyoYoonNam","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam","page":null,"description":"Java, Spring\uc744 \uba54\uc778\uc73c\ub85c \ud558\ub294 \ucde8\uc900\uc0dd\uc785\ub2c8\ub2e4.","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam/","github":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam","email":"mailto:dev.rudevico@gmail.com"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam.png","key":"hyoyoonnam"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"on-parents-and-children","title":"Does Elephant Actually Like Fish Cake? Reading Bacon on Parents and Children","date":"2026-10-19T14:59:21.960Z","authors":"hyoyoonnam","tags":["life"],"image":"../../../../blog/2026-10-19-on-parents-and-children/cover.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"When Indifference Fades | Fitzgerald\u2019s Winter Dreams","permalink":"/en/fading-indifference-in-winter-dreams"},"nextItem":{"title":"Be water, my friend.","permalink":"/en/be-water-my-friend"}},"content":"![cover](../../../../blog/2026-10-19-on-parents-and-children/cover.png)\\n\\nI\u2019m ending the day with a teabag of fish-cake broth. Anyone at Woowacourse could tell you that Elephant, yours truly, doesn\u2019t like fish cake. Especially fish-cake soup.\\n\\nAnd yet today is the sort of day I want to end with that teabag. Does Elephant actually like fish cake?\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n***\\n\\n> To be remembered, to accomplish something, to do noble deeds: these belong to human beings. The noblest works and foundations have come from people without children. Having no image of their bodies to leave behind, they strive to give expression to an image of their minds. \u2014 From the Korean edition\\n\\nI liked the way Bacon starts with the animal instinct to continue a line. If you don\u2019t satisfy it by leaving descendants, an image of your body, he suggests you\u2019ll try to leave an image of your mind.\\n\\nHaving a child is a great thing in itself. It can change what you live for and what you hope to do. And surely it changes how you divide up your hours? People often marry around the time they\u2019re coming into their own physically and at work. If they put marriage aside and poured those hours into their work or research, I can imagine more of what Bacon calls an image of the mind surviving them.\\n\\nThen again, the longer you wait for the right time to marry, the more tempted you are to hold out for the *best* time. Maybe there\u2019s something to marrying when you still don\u2019t feel ready.\\n\\n> Parents would do well to choose their children\u2019s occupations and paths early, when the children are still adaptable. It is possible to defer too much to their preferences in the belief that they should do whatever they please. Where a child shows a remarkable inclination or talent, of course, it is best to let it have its way.\\n\\nDoes he mean that if parents choose early, the child gets to try something sooner and has less to lose by trying something else if it doesn\u2019t fit? I\u2019m going by that bit about being adaptable. I\u2019m finding this one difficult.\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Original post on Rilog (Korean)](https://www.rilog.kr/@homo_scribens/posts/217)"},{"id":"be-water-my-friend","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/be-water-my-friend","editUrl":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam/blog/2026-10-18-be-water-my-friend/index.md","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-18-be-water-my-friend/index.md","title":"Be water, my friend.","description":"Be water, my friend.","date":"2026-10-18T14:37:36.357Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life","description":"Stories from rudevico\u2019s everyday life"}],"readingTime":3.68,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"HyoYoonNam","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam","page":null,"description":"Java, Spring\uc744 \uba54\uc778\uc73c\ub85c \ud558\ub294 \ucde8\uc900\uc0dd\uc785\ub2c8\ub2e4.","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam/","github":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam","email":"mailto:dev.rudevico@gmail.com"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam.png","key":"hyoyoonnam"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"be-water-my-friend","title":"Be water, my friend.","date":"2026-10-18T14:37:36.357Z","authors":"hyoyoonnam","tags":["life"],"image":"../../../../blog/2026-10-18-be-water-my-friend/cover.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Does Elephant Actually Like Fish Cake? Reading Bacon on Parents and Children","permalink":"/en/on-parents-and-children"},"nextItem":{"title":"Francis Bacon on Having No Cause for Anger","permalink":"/en/francis-bacon-on-revenge"}},"content":"![Be water, my friend.](../../../../blog/2026-10-18-be-water-my-friend/cover.jpg)\\n\\n## Becoming water\\n\\n<figure>\\n\\n![image.png](../../../../blog/2026-10-18-be-water-my-friend/rain-warning-ytn.png)\\n\\n<figcaption>1. YTN reports a heavy rain advisory for Seoul and Gyeonggi.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n<figure>\\n\\n![image.png](../../../../blog/2026-10-18-be-water-my-friend/rain-warning-kbs.png)\\n\\n<figcaption>2. The same advisory on KBS.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n*It\u2019s a Sunday in the autumn of 2026, and the rain is coming down hard.* By about six in the evening, though, it was letting up. I started getting my running things together.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nDown at the Tancheon, you could see what the rain had done. The water was just shy of covering the stepping stones between the two banks.\\n\\n<figure>\\n\\n![stepping stones](../../../../blog/2026-10-18-be-water-my-friend/stepping-stones.jpg)\\n\\n<figcaption>3. The stepping stones, just above water.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\nAs if the stream had decided it couldn\u2019t hold much more, the current was picking up, carrying the water off to some place far away.\\n\\n<figure>\\n\\n![image.png](../../../../blog/2026-10-18-be-water-my-friend/river-current.png)\\n\\n<figcaption>4. All that water rushing downstream.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\nI was admiring the way water could flow quietly or pick up speed as the occasion called for it, when something I\u2019d heard once came back to me. A lot of our technology comes from watching what nature does.\\n\\n> It matters to me to have some breathing room in my life. But when things need doing, maybe I can let the current run a little faster for a while. Like water.\\n\\nSo I\u2019ve decided to be water. With so little of my time at Woowacourse left, I think I\u2019m going to need a fairly brisk current.\\n\\n<figure>\\n\\n![biomimetics](../../../../blog/2026-10-18-be-water-my-friend/biomimetics.png)\\n\\n<figcaption>5. Biomimetics, on Wikipedia.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n<figure>\\n\\n![IMG_2278.jpeg](../../../../blog/2026-10-18-be-water-my-friend/run-record.jpg)\\n\\n<figcaption>6. Today\u2019s run.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n### Friends along the Tancheon\\n\\nLast time I ran here I saw raccoon dogs and a bird. I wonder how they fared today.\\n\\n<figure>\\n\\n![IMG_2243.jpeg](../../../../blog/2026-10-18-be-water-my-friend/raccoon-a.jpg)\\n\\n<figcaption>7. Raccoon dog A.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n<figure>\\n\\n![IMG_2246.jpeg](../../../../blog/2026-10-18-be-water-my-friend/raccoon-b.jpg)\\n\\n<figcaption>8. Raccoon dog B.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n<figure>\\n\\n![IMG_2241.jpeg](../../../../blog/2026-10-18-be-water-my-friend/bird.jpg)\\n\\n<figcaption>9. A bird. Asleep, perhaps?</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n<figure>\\n\\n![IMG_2240.jpeg](../../../../blog/2026-10-18-be-water-my-friend/bird-close-up.jpg)\\n\\n<figcaption>10. A closer look.</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n## Reading Bacon\u2019s \u201cOf Adversity\u201d in the Moonye edition\\n\\nThis essay suggests a way to take pleasure in adversity. Several passages circle the same idea, so I\u2019ll finish by gathering them here. The excerpts below are translated from the Korean edition.\\n\\n> The good things of prosperity are worth wishing for. The good things of adversity are worth admiring. \u2014 Seneca\\n\\nI\u2019ve been seeing <strong>[a Korean collection of Seneca for people who feel today slipping away](https://www.yes24.com/product/goods/192474512)</strong> everywhere lately. It was a surprise to run into him in Francis Bacon, too.\\n\\n> Surely, if miracles have power over nature, it\u2019s in adversity that they tend to appear.\\n\\n> The virtue of prosperity is temperance, and the virtue of adversity is fortitude. Of the two, fortitude is by far the more heroic.\\n\\n> Prosperity is the blessing of the Old Testament. Adversity is the blessing of the New. It promises greater grace and reveals God\u2019s protection more clearly.\\n\\n> Even in adversity there is comfort and hope. In needlework or embroidery, how much more pleasing to set a lively pattern against a grave, somber ground than to lay a dark, melancholy pattern on a bright one.\\n\\n> Prosperity brings our vices into view. Adversity brings out our virtues.\\n\\n### Words I had to look up\\n\\n* **Holy Spirit**, also *Holy Ghost*. It\u2019s a Christian term. Even with an explanation in front of me, I find it hard to grasp. I\u2019ve seen Christians in Korea and elsewhere writing about their own confusion over it, too.\\n\\n> The pen of the Holy Spirit also dwells more on the afflictions of Job than on the splendor of Solomon.\\n\\n* **Needlework.** Making something by hand, sometimes with the help of a simple tool.\\n\\n> In needlework or embroidery, to set a lively pattern against a grave, somber ground\u2026\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Original post on Rilog (Korean)](https://www.rilog.kr/@homo_scribens/posts/198)"},{"id":"francis-bacon-on-revenge","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/francis-bacon-on-revenge","editUrl":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam/blog/2026-10-18-francis-bacon-on-revenge/index.md","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-18-francis-bacon-on-revenge/index.md","title":"Francis Bacon on Having No Cause for Anger","description":"cover","date":"2026-10-17T16:07:48.632Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life","description":"Stories from rudevico\u2019s everyday life"}],"readingTime":2.5,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"HyoYoonNam","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam","page":null,"description":"Java, Spring\uc744 \uba54\uc778\uc73c\ub85c \ud558\ub294 \ucde8\uc900\uc0dd\uc785\ub2c8\ub2e4.","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam/","github":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam","email":"mailto:dev.rudevico@gmail.com"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam.png","key":"hyoyoonnam"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"francis-bacon-on-revenge","title":"Francis Bacon on Having No Cause for Anger","date":"2026-10-17T16:07:48.632Z","authors":"hyoyoonnam","tags":["life"],"image":"../../../../blog/2026-10-18-francis-bacon-on-revenge/cover.webp"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Be water, my friend.","permalink":"/en/be-water-my-friend"},"nextItem":{"title":"A Hamburger and The Stranger","permalink":"/en/a-hamburger-and-the-stranger"}},"content":"![cover](../../../../blog/2026-10-18-francis-bacon-on-revenge/cover.webp)\\n\\nWas it five days ago? I bought the Moonye edition of Francis Bacon\u2019s essays. I knew his line about knowledge being power, and that was about all I knew of him. I happened upon the book and wanted to get better acquainted. My other reason was the size of the essays. There are nearly sixty subjects in some 270 pages. One a day sounded like a pleasant little habit.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<figure>\\n\\n![XL](../../../../blog/2026-10-18-francis-bacon-on-revenge/bacon-essays.jpg)\\n\\n<figcaption>https://www.yes24.com/product/goods/147564050</figcaption>\\n</figure>\\n\\n## No cause to be angry with someone?\\n\\n> *Who does another person harm just for the pleasure of doing harm? People do it for some gain or pleasure or honor of their own. Why, then, should I be angry with someone for loving himself more than he loves me?* \u2014 Francis Bacon, \u201cOf Revenge,\u201d translated here from the Korean edition\\n\\nIt reminded me of the saying that if you expect nothing, you won\u2019t get angry. But suppose someone has a bad habit, and I keep quiet because, well, it\u2019s their loss. Isn\u2019t that also a failure to communicate? If we care about each other\u2019s growth, wouldn\u2019t it be better to say something?\\n\\nBacon stops at \u201cthere\u2019s no need to get angry.\u201d He doesn\u2019t offer an alternative like \u201ckeep calm and talk it through.\u201d I\u2019m left wondering how much he includes under anger. Does it cover telling someone off in a temper and giving them ordinary feedback? If I read it that broadly, I\u2019m back where I started. Staying quiet could end up hurting us both.\\n\\n## Words I had to look up\\n\\n* **Gomaehada** (\uace0\ub9e4\ud558\ub2e4). To be distinguished or noble in character, learning, or talent.\\n\\n> By comparison, the spirit of Job speaks in a nobler voice.\\n\\n* **Proverbs.** The biblical book numbered 24th or 20th, depending on the ordering. Solomon is traditionally credited with writing or compiling many of its sayings. These are the names in different languages. I take the word to mean something like a maxim or a piece of counsel.\\n\\n<table>\\n  <tbody>\\n    <tr><th scope=\\"row\\">Hebrew</th><td>\u05de\u05e9\u05dc\u05d9 (Mishlei)</td></tr>\\n    <tr><th scope=\\"row\\">Greek</th><td>\u03a0\u03b1\u03c1\u03bf\u03b9\u03bc\u03af\u03b1\u03b9 (Paroimiai)</td></tr>\\n    <tr><th scope=\\"row\\">Latin</th><td>Proverbia</td></tr>\\n    <tr><th scope=\\"row\\">English</th><td>Proverbs</td></tr>\\n    <tr><th scope=\\"row\\">Korean</th><td>\uc7a0\uc5b8 (\u7bb4\u8a00), literally \u201cwords that prick like a needle\u201d</td></tr>\\n  </tbody>\\n</table>\\n\\n## A little feedback for Rilog\\n\\n### 1. I can\u2019t nest a table under a bullet\\n\\n* A bullet\\n  * A nested bullet\\n* A bullet\\n\\n> I can put a quotation under a bullet.\\n\\n* A bullet\\n\\n<table>\\n  <tbody>\\n    <tr><td>The table won\u2019t go under the bullet.</td><td></td></tr>\\n    <tr><td></td><td></td></tr>\\n  </tbody>\\n</table>\\n\\n### 2. Searching the commands for \u201ctable\u201d turns up nothing\\n\\nWhen I type `/table`, I expect a table to come up. It doesn\u2019t.\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Original post on Rilog (Korean)](https://www.rilog.kr/@homo_scribens/posts/195)"},{"id":"a-hamburger-and-the-stranger","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/a-hamburger-and-the-stranger","editUrl":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam/blog/2026-10-07-a-hamburger-and-the-stranger/index.md","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-07-a-hamburger-and-the-stranger/index.md","title":"A Hamburger and The Stranger","description":"My hamburger at McDonald\u2019s","date":"2026-10-06T18:33:46.544Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Reflections","permalink":"/en/tags/retrospective"}],"readingTime":4.32,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"HyoYoonNam","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam","page":null,"description":"Java, Spring\uc744 \uba54\uc778\uc73c\ub85c \ud558\ub294 \ucde8\uc900\uc0dd\uc785\ub2c8\ub2e4.","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam/","github":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam","email":"mailto:dev.rudevico@gmail.com"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam.png","key":"hyoyoonnam"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"a-hamburger-and-the-stranger","title":"A Hamburger and The Stranger","date":"2026-10-06T18:33:46.544Z","authors":"hyoyoonnam","tags":["\ud68c\uace0"],"image":"../../../../blog/2026-10-07-a-hamburger-and-the-stranger/hamburger.jpg"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Francis Bacon on Having No Cause for Anger","permalink":"/en/francis-bacon-on-revenge"},"nextItem":{"title":"[Backend] Integrating an External API Means Translating Its Contract","permalink":"/en/translating-external-apis"}},"content":"![My hamburger at McDonald\u2019s](../../../../blog/2026-10-07-a-hamburger-and-the-stranger/hamburger.jpg)\\n\\nActually, the hamburger can\u2019t keep me company. I\u2019ve already devoured it without mercy. Then again, maybe it\u2019s closer to me now than it was before I ate it. *What does it matter, anyway?*\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nIt\u2019s 2:50 in the morning. Wednesday, October 7. I\u2019m at the McDonald\u2019s by Sunae Station. There\u2019s something reassuring about a chain that stays open all night. Even when I\u2019m away from home, there\u2019s somewhere I can stay, whatever the hour.\\n\\nWhy am I awake at this hour? And why am I here when I have a perfectly cozy home? Well, here you\u2019ve found me, writing about *The Stranger*. I figured if I was going to work through the small hours anyway, I might as well get something to eat and give myself a change of scenery.\\n\\nI hadn\u2019t actually set out to write about *The Stranger*. I did have to write it eventually. A few of us at Woowacourse had read the book and got together to talk about it, and we\u2019d agreed to bring something we\u2019d written to follow up. But just before I started this, I was making a list of things I\u2019d done, for my application to an entry-level opening at MyRealTrip.\\n\\nThen a couple of thoughts occurred to me.\\n\\n* Looking back over my time at Woowacourse for the application, I could see a resemblance between MyRealTrip and our project. In both, the starting point was the problem we wanted to solve.\\n* If I got the job, what could I do at MyRealTrip with the things I already love?\\n  * I like books. I have a soft spot for films, music, other books, food, even places, once I\u2019ve come across them in a book. Japanese fiction does this to me in particular. People travel around taking photos of places they\u2019ve seen in anime. What if you could plan a route that let you visit places you\u2019d read about, or try the food? I remember reading Haruki Murakami\u2019s *After Dark* and jotting down the things that turned up in it in the back of my copy. A lot of it was music. It was fun. It made me want to build a little wiki of my own as I went through life.\\n\\n![The notes I kept while reading After Dark](../../../../blog/2026-10-07-a-hamburger-and-the-stranger/after-dark-notes.png)\\n\\n* I like running. Travel abroad has never stirred much in me either way. Lately, though, just picturing myself out for a run overseas makes my heart swell. And a good running route is hard to plan. Especially a long one. There are only so many laps of a track you can do before it gets old. It\u2019s tricky enough at home, so I imagine it would be harder in another country. What if we could map out routes in enough detail for people to go running with peace of mind?\\n\\nAs you can probably tell from all that rambling, I got quite excited at the thought of where MyRealTrip was headed and how much fun I might have there. Forming hypotheses, making decisions with data, seeing something through from start to finish even when it took me outside my area. I\u2019d made a point of practicing all that at Woowacourse. I could almost believe those experiences had come my way specifically for MyRealTrip.\\n\\nAnd then there\u2019s this. Would another company recognize what I\u2019m writing down here? Would they see any value in it? The other backend developers in my cohort seem to be preparing for their applications in a rather different way.\\n\\n> *Am I a stranger on the job market?*\\n\\nGetting as far as that question is the beginning and the end of this book report. It\u2019s a question that worries me. It makes me anxious. It\u2019s a challenge, too. I\u2019ve got all these names for the situation, and I don\u2019t know quite what to do with any of them.\\n\\nEven this is a hypothesis. It needs testing. The weight of it is rather different from that of a hypothesis in a project or a survey. A good deal heavier. Still, it\u2019s mine to bear.\\n\\n> *Is this life? Good. Then I\u2019ll do it again.*\\n\\nI\u2019ve written a book report without saying what happens in *The Stranger*. Yet I feel closer to Meursault now than I did when I still had the whole book fresh in my head, scribbling in the margins about what a scene was doing or what a sentence stood for.\\n\\nThe way I used to read was to get a feel for the people in a book, for the time they lived in, and to enjoy being there. People say books let you learn about other people\u2019s lives. And yet, as far as Meursault\u2019s own life goes, I feel further away from it now. I don\u2019t know what the book is about. I don\u2019t know what it\u2019s about, but right now, I\u2019m a stranger.\\n\\n### *3:33 a.m. This is where I stop.*\\n\\n![After the meal](../../../../blog/2026-10-07-a-hamburger-and-the-stranger/after-the-meal.jpg)\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Original post on Rilog (Korean)](https://www.rilog.kr/@rudevico/posts/155)"},{"id":"translating-external-apis","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/translating-external-apis","editUrl":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam/blog/2026-08-28-translating-external-apis/index.md","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-08-28-translating-external-apis/index.md","title":"[Backend] Integrating an External API Means Translating Its Contract","description":"* How mapping data between DTOs can involve translating between two different API contracts.","date":"2026-08-28T03:19:38.489Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Technology","permalink":"/en/tags/tech"}],"readingTime":6.47,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"HyoYoonNam","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam","page":null,"description":"Java, Spring\uc744 \uba54\uc778\uc73c\ub85c \ud558\ub294 \ucde8\uc900\uc0dd\uc785\ub2c8\ub2e4.","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam/","github":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam","email":"mailto:dev.rudevico@gmail.com"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam.png","key":"hyoyoonnam"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"translating-external-apis","title":"[Backend] Integrating an External API Means Translating Its Contract","date":"2026-08-28T03:19:38.489Z","authors":"hyoyoonnam","tags":["\uae30\uc220"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"A Hamburger and The Stranger","permalink":"/en/a-hamburger-and-the-stranger"},"nextItem":{"title":"Learning to Read with SQ3R","permalink":"/en/explanation-about-SQ3R"}},"content":":::note[What you\u2019ll take away]\\n\\n* How mapping data between DTOs can involve translating between two different API contracts.\\n* Where to translate external data, and when it makes sense to move that work closer to the external system.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## 1. Do separate DTOs make us independent of the external API?\\n\\nChaekchaek uses Aladin\u2019s Search API to look up books. From the outset, we had one data transfer object (DTO) for the response we received from Aladin and another for the response we returned to our client.\\n\\n```text\\nAladin Search API\\n        \u2193     (enter our backend)\\nAladinSearchResponse\\n        \u2193\\nBookSearchResponse\\n        \u2193     (leave our backend)\\nChaekchaek Client\\n```\\n\\nOn paper, that gives the two APIs a clean separation. Yet fields from Aladin\u2019s pagination model showed up in our public response almost word for word.\\n\\n```java\\npublic record BookSearchResponse(\\n        int totalResults,\\n        int startIndex,\\n        int itemsPerPage,\\n        boolean hasNext,\\n        List<BookItem> items\\n) {\\n}\\n```\\n\\nWe had two objects, but **our public contract still reflected the way Aladin represented its data**. A client using Chaekchaek should only have to learn Chaekchaek\u2019s contract. Splitting the DTOs had left that part of the job unfinished.\\n\\n***\\n\\n## 2. The provider and our service speak different languages\\n\\nEven two providers offering the same book search can describe a result quite differently.\\n\\n| Meaning | Aladin | Another provider |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Search results | `item` | `documents` |\\n| Authors | `author: String`, including contributor roles | `authors: String[]` |\\n| Translators | Included in `author` | `translators: String[]` |\\n| Next page | Calculated from pagination fields | A separate flag such as `is_end` |\\n\\nThe differences go deeper than field names. Providers decide how to divide the same information into fields and how to represent state. Each model makes sense in its own context. **Aladin has a model for its service, and Chaekchaek needs one for ours.**\\n\\nIn practice, we made three kinds of translation.\\n\\n1. We adopted our own terms. For the number of books found, `totalResults` became `totalCount`.\\n2. We gave loosely structured data a shape our client could use. One string containing authors and translators became a separate array for each role.\\n3. We turned the provider\u2019s state into the client\u2019s next action. The current page number helped us work out which page to request next.\\n\\nThe last two in particular required a decision about **which information matters to our service**.\\n\\n***\\n\\n## 3. The external DTO keeps the provider\u2019s vocabulary\\n\\nThat raises a fair question.\\n\\n> If our service needs authors and translators separately, why don\u2019t we split them as soon as we receive the response? Do we really need both `AladinSearchResponse` and `BookSearchResponse`?\\n\\nTechnically, we could do that. I\u2019d still keep a DTO specifically for Aladin\u2019s response. If you\u2019re familiar with SOLID, the single responsibility principle gives a good reason. Consider what would make us change each model:\\n\\n```text\\nAladinSearchResponse changes\\n\u2192 when Aladin changes its API contract\\n\\nChaekchaek\u2019s search result model changes\\n\u2192 when Chaekchaek\u2019s requirements change\\n```\\n\\nThose are two different responsibilities. Keeping them separate gives us somewhere to absorb a change in Aladin\u2019s API before it reaches our clients.\\n\\n***\\n\\n## 4. What the translation actually does\\n\\nTwo parts of the implementation made this especially clear.\\n\\n### 4.1. Turning contributor credits into structured data\\n\\nAladin puts authors and translators in a single string:\\n\\n```text\\n\uc564\ub514 \uc704\uc5b4 (\uc9c0\uc740\uc774), \ubc15\uc544\ub78c (\uc62e\uae34\uc774)\\n```\\n\\nHere, `\uc9c0\uc740\uc774` marks the author and `\uc62e\uae34\uc774` marks the translator. Our client needs to use those roles separately:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"authors\\": [\\"\uc564\ub514 \uc704\uc5b4\\"],\\n  \\"translators\\": [\\"\ubc15\uc544\ub78c\\"]\\n}\\n```\\n\\nI wrote an `AladinContributorParser` to interpret the string. Along the way I found a case where splitting on commas would give the wrong answer:\\n\\n```text\\n\ubbfc\uad00\uc2dd (\ud544\uba85, \uccad\uc6d0) (\uc9c0\uc740\uc774)\\n```\\n\\nThe comma inside the parentheses introduces a pen name. It doesn\u2019t separate two contributors.\\n\\nWe had to interpret Aladin\u2019s conventions to get a structure Chaekchaek could use. That also meant deciding what to keep. Suppose our service uses only authors and translators and drops illustrators (`\uadf8\ub9bc`), editorial supervisors (`\uac10\uc218`), and compilers (`\uc5ee\uc740\uc774`). That\u2019s a product decision, too.\\n\\n> **The information we allow a translation to lose is part of our policy.**\\n\\n### 4.2. Turning pagination state into a next action\\n\\nAladin describes pagination with three fields:\\n\\n```text\\ntotalResults\\nstartIndex\\nitemsPerPage\\n```\\n\\nAt first, we exposed those fields alongside `hasNext`. To fetch another page, the client had to know the rule:\\n\\n```js\\nconst nextPage = hasNext ? startIndex + 1 : null;\\n```\\n\\nOur server already understood Aladin\u2019s pagination model. It made sense to do the calculation there. That gave us a simpler public response:\\n\\n```java\\npublic record BookSearchResponse(\\n        int totalCount,\\n        Integer nextPage,\\n        List<BookItem> items\\n) {\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe client can now use the value of `nextPage` directly:\\n\\n```text\\nnextPage = 3\\n\u2192 Request page 3 next.\\n\\nnextPage = null\\n\u2192 There are no more pages.\\n```\\n\\nThe distinction between these fields is easy to miss. `hasNext` describes **the current state**. `nextPage` gives the client **the value it needs to act**. The client no longer needs to interpret `startIndex` and `itemsPerPage` or work out the page number itself.\\n\\n> **We translated the provider\u2019s state into the next action our client could take.**\\n\\n***\\n\\n## 5. Where should the translation happen?\\n\\nAt the time, we did the translation in `BookSearchService`:\\n\\n```text\\nAladin Search API\\n        \u2193\\nAladinBookClient\\n        \u2193\\nAladinSearchResponse\\n        \u2193\\nBookSearchService\\n  - Structure the contributor credits\\n  - Calculate nextPage\\n  - Convert fields\\n        \u2193\\nBookSearchResponse\\n        \u2193\\nChaekchaek Client\\n```\\n\\nThis kept Aladin\u2019s field names and pagination rules out of the client. Inside the backend, though, `BookSearchService` still depended directly on provider types such as `AladinSearchResponse` and `AladinBookItem`. **The external contract reached as far as our application service.**\\n\\nThat was a reasonable trade-off at the time. We had one search provider, and getting search working mattered more. Abstractions have a cost of their own. What I want to understand about a boundary is **what it would cost us to make a change with that boundary in place**.\\n\\nIf adding or replacing a provider became a realistic prospect, we could finish the translation before the response reached the application service:\\n\\n```text\\nAladin Search API\\n        \u2193\\nAladin Adapter\\n  - Interpret the external contract\\n  - Structure the contributor credits\\n  - Calculate pagination\\n        \u2193\\nBookSearchResult\\n        \u2193\\nBookSearchService\\n```\\n\\nBy the time the service receives the result, it\u2019s already in Chaekchaek\u2019s terms. The service can use it without knowing who supplied it or how they encoded it. The particular interface or adapter pattern is secondary here.\\n\\n> **Where a boundary belongs can change as the pressures on the system change.**\\n\\n***\\n\\n## 6. A change in the response can break the translation\\n\\nOur `AladinContributorParser` understands credits like this one:\\n\\n```text\\n\uc564\ub514 \uc704\uc5b4 (\uc9c0\uc740\uc774), \ubc15\uc544\ub78c (\uc62e\uae34\uc774)\\n```\\n\\nIts knowledge of labels such as `(\uc9c0\uc740\uc774)` and `(\uc62e\uae34\uc774)` comes with an assumption: **Aladin will keep sending credits in a format we recognize.**\\n\\nWhat if a new role such as `\uc5ee\uc740\uc774` appears? What if the same information arrives as `\uc9c0\uc740\uc774=\uc564\ub514 \uc704\uc5b4, ...`? A missing JSON field might cause deserialization to fail immediately. The harder case is when the code runs successfully and produces the wrong result.\\n\\nIf the parser credits someone as an author by mistake, or silently discards a role it doesn\u2019t recognize, the request can still succeed. **The client receives an incorrect result that looks perfectly ordinary.** How to detect a change upstream, what to do with a value we can\u2019t interpret, and how much to guard against are questions worth a separate post.\\n\\n***\\n\\n## 7. Integrating an external API means translating\\n\\nWhen I started, I pictured the work like this:\\n\\n```text\\nAladin response\\n\u2192 Put it in a DTO\\n\u2192 Pick the fields we need and return them\\n```\\n\\nWhat I ended up doing was different. We read two distinct roles out of a single contributor credit:\\n\\n```text\\n\uc564\ub514 \uc704\uc5b4 (\uc9c0\uc740\uc774), \ubc15\uc544\ub78c (\uc62e\uae34\uc774)\\n\\n\u2193\\n\\nauthors: [\\"\uc564\ub514 \uc704\uc5b4\\"]\\ntranslators: [\\"\ubc15\uc544\ub78c\\"]\\n```\\n\\nWe also turned the pagination fields into a value the client could use directly:\\n\\n```text\\ntotalResults + startIndex + itemsPerPage\\n\\n\u2193\\n\\nnextPage\\n```\\n\\nSeen from the client\u2019s side, that same change looks like this:\\n\\n```text\\nAladin\u2019s pagination data\\ntotalResults + startIndex + itemsPerPage\\n\\n\u2193\\n\\nThe value the client will use\\nnextPage\\n```\\n\\nIn both cases, the work was in deciding **what the provider\u2019s data meant for our service**. That\u2019s what I mean by translation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Original post on Rilog (Korean)](https://www.rilog.kr/@chaekchaek/posts/23)"},{"id":"explanation-about-SQ3R","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/explanation-about-SQ3R","editUrl":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam/blog/2026-03-17-SQ3R\uc774\ub780/index.md","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-03-17-SQ3R\uc774\ub780/index.md","title":"Learning to Read with SQ3R","description":"SQ3R is a way to study as you read.","date":"2026-03-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Practicing SQ3R","permalink":"/en/tags/sq-3-r-\uc758\uc2dd\uc801\uc73c\ub85c-\uc5f0\uc2b5\ud558\uae30"}],"readingTime":1.8,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"HyoYoonNam","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam","page":null,"description":"Java, Spring\uc744 \uba54\uc778\uc73c\ub85c \ud558\ub294 \ucde8\uc900\uc0dd\uc785\ub2c8\ub2e4.","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam/","github":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam","email":"mailto:dev.rudevico@gmail.com"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam.png","key":"hyoyoonnam"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"explanation-about-SQ3R","title":"Learning to Read with SQ3R","authors":"hyoyoonnam","tags":["SQ3R_\uc758\uc2dd\uc801\uc73c\ub85c_\uc5f0\uc2b5\ud558\uae30"],"status":"\ud83d\udfe9_done","topics":["[[woowa]]","[[Lv 1]]"],"started":"2026-03-17T00:00:00.000Z","deadline(or finished)":"2026-03-17T15:37:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"[Backend] Integrating an External API Means Translating Its Contract","permalink":"/en/translating-external-apis"},"nextItem":{"title":"Unmodifiable Lists and Immutable Lists in Java","permalink":"/en/difference-between-collections-unmodifiablelist-and-List-of-immutablelist"}},"content":"**SQ3R** is a way to study as you read.\\n\\nThe idea is to read deliberately, coming up with your own questions and looking for answers as you go.\\n\\n## How I came across it\\n\\nI used the sources below to put together an account of what SQ3R is and how to try it.\\n\\nI first encountered it in [Growing Together, by Kim Chang-joon](https://product.kyobobook.co.kr/detail/S000001033071). Lately I\u2019ve wanted to get better at picking up new things quickly and effectively, so I decided to look into it and give it a try.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Survey\\n\\nHold off on reading the text itself. Look at the contents, titles, and subheadings to get a sense of the whole.\\n\\n## Question\\n\\nWrite down questions about anything you couldn\u2019t understand or anticipate during the survey.\\n\\nFor instance, what might a chapter called \u201cThe Programming Language Master\u201d cover? Will it just tell me how good the expert is? Will they share how they got there? What would I have to do to get that good myself?\\n\\n:::note\\nReading with your own questions in mind gives you somewhere to direct your attention.\\n:::\\n\\n## Read\\n\\nHighlight or annotate the passages that bear on the questions you picked out. Give your eyes something to come back to.\\n\\n## Recite\\n\\nAfter reading the parts that address a question, try answering it out loud in your own words. With a bit of luck, your reading has given you what you need.\\n\\nMake a note of any questions you struggle to answer. You\u2019ll use them in the last step.\\n\\n## Review\\n\\nGo back through the questions you wrote down earlier.\\n\\nKeep the questions physically separate from what you read. Quiz cards are one way to do it.\\n\\nWith the questions and the text side by side, you can pick up clues without noticing and mistake recognition for knowing the answer.\\n\\nGive yourself the harder job of pulling it out of memory. After all, you generally can\u2019t look it up during an exam! (Well, there are open-book exams\u2026)\\n\\n## References\\n- [Growing Together, by Kim Chang-joon](https://product.kyobobook.co.kr/detail/S000001033071), the chapter on mastering a programming language\\n- [SQ3R - Harvard Univ.](https://academicresourcecenter.harvard.edu/2024/03/27/sq3r/)"},{"id":"difference-between-collections-unmodifiablelist-and-List-of-immutablelist","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/difference-between-collections-unmodifiablelist-and-List-of-immutablelist","editUrl":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam/blog/2026-03-13-unmodifiable-list\uc640-immutable-list/index.md","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-03-13-unmodifiable-list\uc640-immutable-list/index.md","title":"Unmodifiable Lists and Immutable Lists in Java","description":"1. How a view and a snapshot explain the difference between an unmodifiable collection and an immutable one.","date":"2026-03-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Java","permalink":"/en/tags/java"}],"readingTime":2.81,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"HyoYoonNam","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam","page":null,"description":"Java, Spring\uc744 \uba54\uc778\uc73c\ub85c \ud558\ub294 \ucde8\uc900\uc0dd\uc785\ub2c8\ub2e4.","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam/","github":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam","email":"mailto:dev.rudevico@gmail.com"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam.png","key":"hyoyoonnam"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"difference-between-collections-unmodifiablelist-and-List-of-immutablelist","title":"Unmodifiable Lists and Immutable Lists in Java","authors":"hyoyoonnam","tags":["Java"],"status":"\ud83d\udfe9_done","topics":["[[woowa]]","[[Lv 1]]","[[Java]]"],"started":"2026-03-13T00:00:00.000Z","deadline(or finished)":"2026-03-16T22:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Learning to Read with SQ3R","permalink":"/en/explanation-about-SQ3R"},"nextItem":{"title":"Sending a Message and Calling a Method","permalink":"/en/difference-between-message-sending-and-method-calling"}},"content":":::note[What you\u2019ll take away]\\n\\n1. How a view and a snapshot explain the difference between an unmodifiable collection and an immutable one.\\n\\n2. How the lists produced by these calls differ:\\n    - `Collections.unmodifiableList()`\\n    - `List.of()` or `List.copyOf()`\\n\\n:::\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n# 1. What they have in common\\n\\nCalling a mutating method throws an `UnsupportedOperationException`.\\n\\n<iframe\\n  frameBorder=\\"0\\"\\n  src=\\"https://onecompiler.com/java/44gg6wqev?codeEditors=true&hideNew=true&hideNewFile=true\\"\\n  width=\\"100%\\"\\n  height=\\"600px\\"\\n  title=\\"Unmodifiable vs Immutable List Java Example\\">\\n</iframe>\\n\\n# 2. Where they differ\\n\\n## 2.1. They use different implementation classes\\n\\n<iframe\\n  frameBorder=\\"0\\"\\n  src=\\"https://onecompiler.com/java/44gh4m5rq?codeEditors=true&hideNew=true&hideNewFile=true\\"\\n  width=\\"100%\\"\\n  height=\\"600px\\"\\n  title=\\"Unmodifiable vs Immutable List Java Example\\">\\n</iframe>\\n\\n## 2.2. They have different relationships to the original collection\\n\\n### UnmodifiableList\\n\\nThe `UnmodifiableRandomAccessList` implementation (which I\u2019ll call `UnmodifiableList` here) is a **view** of the original collection.\\n\\nAs the code below shows, it holds the original `list` in a field. For a read like `get`, the wrapper delegates to the corresponding method on that list and returns the result.\\n\\nSay `x001` refers to the original list and `x002` to the view. You can call `add` through `x001`. Trying it through `x002` throws.\\n\\nA change made through `x001` also shows up through `x002`, since the wrapper still holds that same list.\\n\\n```java\\nstatic class UnmodifiableList<E> extends UnmodifiableCollection<E>\\n                              implements List<E> {\\n    final List<? extends E> list;\\n\\n    UnmodifiableList(List<? extends E> list) {\\n        super(list);\\n        this.list = list;\\n    }\\n\\n    ...\\n\\n    public E get(int index) {return list.get(index);}\\n\\n    public void add(int index, E element) {\\n        throw new UnsupportedOperationException();\\n    }\\n    ...\\n}\\n```\\n\\n<br></br>\\n\\nYou can try it in this example.\\n\\n<iframe\\n  frameBorder=\\"0\\"\\n  src=\\"https://onecompiler.com/java/44gh57eya?codeEditors=true&hideNew=true&hideNewFile=true\\"\\n  width=\\"100%\\"\\n  height=\\"600px\\"\\n  title=\\"Unmodifiable vs Immutable List Java Example\\">\\n</iframe>\\n\\n### ImmutableList\\n\\nThis list captures the contents of the original collection at the moment of the copy. Later structural changes to the original list don\u2019t affect it.\\n\\n```java\\n// List.java\\n...\\nstatic <E> List<E> copyOf(Collection<? extends E> coll) {\\n    return ImmutableCollections.listCopy(coll);\\n}\\n...\\n```\\n\\n```java\\n// ImmutableCollections.java\\n...\\nstatic <E> List<E> listCopy(Collection<? extends E> coll) {\\n    ...\\n    } else {\\n        // Copy the elements into a collection independent of the original.\\n        return (List<E>)List.of(coll.toArray());\\n    }\\n}\\n...\\n```\\n\\n# 3. When List.copyOf() can return the list it was given\\n\\nI left out a branch earlier to keep the example manageable. Here\u2019s the full body of `ImmutableCollections.listCopy()`. Look at the first `if`.\\n\\n```java\\nstatic <E> List<E> listCopy(Collection<? extends E> coll) {\\n    // List12 and ListN both extend AbstractImmutableList.\\n    // For these compatible immutable lists, copyOf can return the input itself.\\n    if (coll instanceof List12 || (coll instanceof ListN<?> c && !c.allowNulls)) {\\n        return (List<E>)coll;\\n    // The remaining branches handle other inputs.\\n    } else if (coll.isEmpty()) {\\n        return List.of();\\n    } else {\\n        return (List<E>)List.of(coll.toArray());\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n<br></br>\\n\\nYou can also find this in the Javadoc\u2019s implementation note:\\n\\n:::note[Implementation Note:]\\n\\nIf the given Collection is an unmodifiable List, calling copyOf will generally not create a copy.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n<br></br>\\n\\nIn the next example, compare the references. For this immutable list, `List.copyOf` returns the same object.\\n\\n<iframe\\n  frameBorder=\\"0\\"\\n  src=\\"https://onecompiler.com/embed/java/44gg6962y?codeEditors=true&hideNew=true&hideNewFile=true\\"\\n  width=\\"100%\\"\\n  height=\\"500px\\">\\n</iframe>\\n\\nThe last example times repeated calls with the same immutable input. There\u2019s no copying to do, and the elapsed time shown in this run is 0 ms.\\n\\n<iframe\\n  frameBorder=\\"0\\"\\n  src=\\"https://onecompiler.com/embed/java/44gh5xgjx?codeEditors=true&hideNew=true&hideNewFile=true\\"\\n  width=\\"100%\\"\\n  height=\\"500px\\">\\n</iframe>\\n\\n## References"},{"id":"difference-between-message-sending-and-method-calling","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/difference-between-message-sending-and-method-calling","editUrl":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam/blog/2026-01-18-message\uc640-operation\uc740-\ub2e4\ub974\ub2e4/index.md","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-01-18-message\uc640-operation\uc740-\ub2e4\ub974\ub2e4/index.md","title":"Sending a Message and Calling a Method","description":"We often say that we call a method. Seen through the lens of object-oriented programming, a more precise description of what the sender does is send a message.","date":"2026-01-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Object-oriented programming","permalink":"/en/tags/object-oriented","description":"Posts about object-oriented programming"}],"readingTime":1.73,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"HyoYoonNam","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam","page":null,"description":"Java, Spring\uc744 \uba54\uc778\uc73c\ub85c \ud558\ub294 \ucde8\uc900\uc0dd\uc785\ub2c8\ub2e4.","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam/","github":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam","email":"mailto:dev.rudevico@gmail.com"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam.png","key":"hyoyoonnam"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"difference-between-message-sending-and-method-calling","title":"Sending a Message and Calling a Method","authors":"hyoyoonnam","tags":["\uac1d\uccb4\uc9c0\ud5a5"],"started":"2026-01-18T00:00:00.000Z","deadline(or finished)":null},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Unmodifiable Lists and Immutable Lists in Java","permalink":"/en/difference-between-collections-unmodifiablelist-and-List-of-immutablelist"},"nextItem":{"title":"I Won Inflearn\u2019s 2025 Answer Award!","permalink":"/en/inflearn-2025-end-of-year-awards"}},"content":"We often say that we *call a method*. Seen through the lens of object-oriented programming, a more precise description of what the sender does is *send a message*.\\n\\nI learned this in Java, a statically typed language, where the two are easy to conflate. Comparing it with JavaScript helped me see the distinction.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. A little terminology\\n\\n```java\\n// The return type, name, and parameters are part of the declaration. In discussing the interface, we\u2019ll refer to the operation and its signature.\\npublic void sayHello(String name) {\\n\\t// The body supplies the implementation, also called the method.\\n\\tSystem.out.println(\\"\uc548\ub155 \\" + name + \\"!\\");\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## 1. Java checks types before the program runs\\n\\nJava is statically typed. The compiler checks the type at compile time.\\nWhen the sender tries to send a message, the receiver\u2019s declared type has to expose the corresponding operation. Otherwise, the program never gets to send it.\\nCompilation fails.\\n\\nWorking in a language like Java, it\u2019s easy to come away thinking that sending a message and calling a method are the same thing.\\n\\n<iframe\\n frameBorder=\\"0\\"\\n src=\\"https://onecompiler.com/embed/java/44axz2pcq?codeEditors=true&hideNew=true&hideNewFile=true\\"\\n width=\\"100%\\"\\n height=\\"500px\\">\\n</iframe>\\n\\n## 2. JavaScript checks at runtime\\n\\nJavaScript is dynamically typed. There\u2019s no compile-time check that a `sayHello()` method exists on the receiver.\\nYou can try sending the message to a `rock` object that has no such method. Ordinarily, trying to call it will then fail at runtime.\\n\\n<iframe\\n frameBorder=\\"0\\"\\n src=\\"https://onecompiler.com/embed/java/44axefzbe?codeEditors=true&hideNew=true&hideNewFile=true\\"\\n width=\\"100%\\"\\n height=\\"500px\\">\\n</iframe>\\n\\n### A receiver can even respond without defining the named method\\n\\nIt\u2019s hard to explain the next example as a call to a method on `rock`. There\u2019s no `sayHello()` implementation sitting on that object.\\n\\nThe message model makes it easier to follow. The sender sends a message named `sayHello`. The receiver intercepts it and decides how to respond at runtime.\\n\\n<iframe\\n frameBorder=\\"0\\"\\n src=\\"https://onecompiler.com/embed/java/44ay38vb2?codeEditors=true&hideNew=true&hideNewFile=true\\"\\n width=\\"100%\\"\\n height=\\"500px\\">\\n</iframe>\\n\\n## References\\n*Objects*, Cho Young-ho, p. 49."},{"id":"inflearn-2025-end-of-year-awards","metadata":{"permalink":"/en/inflearn-2025-end-of-year-awards","editUrl":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam/blog/2025-12-24-\uc778\ud504\ub7f0-\uc5f0\ub9d0-\uc5b4\uc6cc\ub4dc-\uc218\uc0c1/index.md","source":"@site/i18n/en/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2025-12-24-\uc778\ud504\ub7f0-\uc5f0\ub9d0-\uc5b4\uc6cc\ub4dc-\uc218\uc0c1/index.md","title":"I Won Inflearn\u2019s 2025 Answer Award!","description":"my-award","date":"2025-12-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Life","permalink":"/en/tags/life","description":"Stories from rudevico\u2019s everyday life"}],"readingTime":4.02,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"HyoYoonNam","title":"Software Engineer","url":"https://linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam","page":null,"description":"Java, Spring\uc744 \uba54\uc778\uc73c\ub85c \ud558\ub294 \ucde8\uc900\uc0dd\uc785\ub2c8\ub2e4.","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/hyoyoonnam/","github":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam","email":"mailto:dev.rudevico@gmail.com"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/hyoyoonnam.png","key":"hyoyoonnam"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"inflearn-2025-end-of-year-awards","title":"I Won Inflearn\u2019s 2025 Answer Award!","authors":"hyoyoonnam","tags":["life"],"image":"./my-award.png","started":"2025-12-24T00:00:00.000Z","deadline(or finished)":"2025-12-24T15:00:00.000Z","status":"\ud83d\udfe9_done"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Sending a Message and Calling a Method","permalink":"/en/difference-between-message-sending-and-method-calling"}},"content":"![my-award](./my-award.png)\\n\\nInflearn picked me for its end-of-year award for answering questions!\\n\\n![jerry](https://media1.tenor.com/m/wOlC5m7NikkAAAAd/%EC%A0%9C%EB%A6%AC%EC%9D%B8%EC%82%AC-%EC%A1%B4%EC%A4%91.gif)\\n\\nYes, that\u2019s me. I\u2019d like to share this award with the production team, who worked so hard, and with\u2026 well, you know the speech.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n---\\n\\n## How the gift found its way to me\\n![inflearn-awards-2025](../../../../blog/2025-12-24-\uc778\ud504\ub7f0-\uc5f0\ub9d0-\uc5b4\uc6cc\ub4dc-\uc218\uc0c1/inflearn-awards-2025.png)\\nI didn\u2019t even know Inflearn had end-of-year awards. When an email came asking for my address, I took it for one of their regular promotional emails and went right past it.\\n\\nLuckily for me, someone at Inflearn thought to follow up by text. I got my address in after all.\\n\\n![present-1](../../../../blog/2025-12-24-\uc778\ud504\ub7f0-\uc5f0\ub9d0-\uc5b4\uc6cc\ub4dc-\uc218\uc0c1/present-1.jpg)\\n![present-2](../../../../blog/2025-12-24-\uc778\ud504\ub7f0-\uc5f0\ub9d0-\uc5b4\uc6cc\ub4dc-\uc218\uc0c1/present-2.jpg)\\nAnd what arrived? Wine and cheese! Just the thing for us grown-up kids studying hard on Inflearn.\\n\\n![present-3](../../../../blog/2025-12-24-\uc778\ud504\ub7f0-\uc5f0\ub9d0-\uc5b4\uc6cc\ub4dc-\uc218\uc0c1/present-3.jpg)\\n\\n## Why did I start answering questions?\\nI learn a surprising amount from answering other people\u2019s questions. And I enjoy the answering itself.\\n\\nThere\u2019s quite a distance between knowing something and helping someone else understand it. Sometimes I wonder how well I know a thing if I can only explain it to myself.\\n\\nI\u2019ve often started typing with a cheerful \u201cOh, I know this one! Let me explain,\u201d\\nonly to stop halfway through. \u201cI\u2019ve always taken this bit for granted. How would I explain it to this person?\u201d Or, \u201cIs that actually true? Where does the official documentation say so?\u201d\\n\\nThat\u2019s the weight of telling someone how something works.\\n\\nTo finish the answer, I have to put what I know into words someone else can follow and find evidence for it. In the process I fill in gaps I hadn\u2019t realized were there.\\n\\n## A soft spot for Inflearn\\n![smile](../../../../blog/2025-12-24-\uc778\ud504\ub7f0-\uc5f0\ub9d0-\uc5b4\uc6cc\ub4dc-\uc218\uc0c1/smile.gif)\\n\\nI already liked Inflearn and was happy using it. I can hardly pretend an end-of-year present hasn\u2019t made me a little fonder of it.\\n\\nInflearn gave me a chance to leave them a message, so I tried to fit all that affection into it. I\u2019ll leave it here to close out the post.\\n\\n---\\n\\n#### A note to Inflearn\\n> I had no idea you did anything like this. What a lovely way to end the year!\\n>\\n> For the past couple of months I\u2019ve been at the Baemin bootcamp, working on the fundamentals as I prepare to look for a job. I haven\u2019t been on Inflearn as much, so it was lovely to be included on the strength of the answers I\u2019d been posting before that.\\n>\\n> I kept volunteering answers for two reasons. Writing them helped me, and I thought someone might get as much out of mine as I\u2019d gotten out of other people\u2019s.\\n>\\n> Other students\u2019 answers have often left me with a new question. Chasing it down helped me grow, and sometimes one answer gave me a way of seeing something that hadn\u2019t occurred to me at all.\\n>\\n> I started answering to return a little of that kindness. Of course, having to explain something to someone else helped me work on my own weak spots, too.\\n>\\n> Inflearn was there when I first started learning to program. I\u2019m pretty attached to it.\\n>\\n> Over the last few years I\u2019ve watched the mobile app get updates, new features arrive, and events come and go. I catch myself thinking, \u201cThey\u2019re really working at this. Look how much room they still have to grow.\u201d It makes me oddly proud.\\n>\\n> I\u2019ve found every course I needed on Inflearn. I haven\u2019t even signed up for another service, though I think there are two or three others in Korea.\\n>\\n> I\u2019m not sure what you currently offer new members. A generous welcome offer might be worth a try. Once people start taking courses, I can see them sticking around.\\n>\\n> Another way to reach new students might be to get in touch with university administrators or student councils and set up a little campus booth with courses or coupons to give away.\\n>\\n> They\u2019re in different businesses, but Notion recruits student ambassadors, and I\u2019ve often seen Monster giving away energy drinks on campus.\\n>\\n> Another thought as I write this: if a few instructors were interested, perhaps Inflearn could approach a university about hosting a guest lecture. I can imagine that going over quite well.\\n>\\n> This has turned into a long note. I hope you have a lovely end to the year, and that in 2026 both Inflearn and I get a little closer to the things we\u2019re working toward!"}]}}')}}]);